\chapter{Introduction}

\texttt{thepulse-markets} is a Common Lisp client for the Pulse Verity
Index, the read API documented at
\url{https://thepulse.markets/developers}.  It is not an official Pulse
product.  The calls go to

\begin{center}
\url{https://mcp.thepulse.markets/api/index/v1/}
\end{center}

Pulse publishes a USD index for crypto tickers.  A price response carries
an ECDSA P-256 signature over four fields: the symbol, the price exactly
as text, the timestamp, and the grade.  The library checks that signature
locally against the public key ring.  It does not ask Pulse whether a
stored print is still good.

The thin layer lives in \texttt{thepulse-markets/rest-api}: bearer auth,
429 retries that stop for a monthly cap, JSON via Yason, and one function
per documented REST read.  The \texttt{thepulse-markets} package
re-exports that layer and adds the helpers you actually want:
\texttt{price}, \texttt{verify-print}, \texttt{ohlc-history} as a list of
\texttt{ohlc-bar} objects, and \texttt{ohlc-bars-for-ingest} for
candlesticks~\cite{candlesticks}.

The shape follows \texttt{cl-coingecko}~\cite{cl-coingecko} and
\texttt{yahoo-finance}~\cite{yahoo-finance}.  Specs are
\texttt{sigma/behave} forms and run when the system loads.

The WebSocket stream is not wrapped.  The developer console (keys,
billing, webhooks) is a browser session on \texttt{thepulse.markets},
not this API key, and is not wrapped either.
